    <string name="h_br_add_1_a">Unlike denominators cannot be added directly.</string>
    <string name="h_br_add_1_b">Give both fractions a common denominator, then you only add the numerators.</string>
    <string name="h_br_add_1_c">The common denominator is %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_br_div_1_a">Dividing by a fraction can be turned into a multiplication.</string>
    <string name="h_br_div_1_b">Flip the second fraction and multiply.</string>
    <string name="h_br_div_1_c">The reciprocal of %1$s is %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_br_kue_1_a">Which number divides both numerator and denominator?</string>
    <string name="h_br_kue_1_b">Check 2, 3 and 5 in turn and reduce until nothing more works.</string>
    <string name="h_br_kue_1_c">Both are divisible by %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_br_mul_1_a">You do not need a common denominator here.</string>
    <string name="h_br_mul_1_b">Multiply numerator by numerator and denominator by denominator.</string>
    <string name="h_br_mul_1_c">Numerator: %1$s · %2$s = %3$s</string>
    <string name="h_dz_add_1_a">First look at where the decimal point sits in both numbers.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_add_1_b">Pad the shorter number with a zero so the places line up.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_add_1_c">%1$s and %2$s, now place by place.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_bru_1_a">A fraction is nothing more than a division.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_bru_1_b">Expand to 10, 100 or 1000, then you can read off the decimal number.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_bru_1_c">Expand by %1$s to the denominator %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_mul_1_a">First compute the task without any decimal point.</string>
    <string name="h_dz_mul_1_b">Add up the decimal places of both numbers; the result has that many.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_a">First decide whether an area or a volume is wanted.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_b">The area of a rectangle is length times width.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_c">Work out %1$s · %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_d">For a triangle, halving follows the multiplication.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_e">Base times height gives %1$s. Now halve it.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_f">For a cuboid, all three edges are multiplied.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_g">The base area is %1$s, the height is still missing.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_h">Work out the whole cuboid first, then the cut-out, then the difference.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_koe_1_i">The whole cuboid has %1$s. The cut-out comes off that.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_pyt_1_a">Sketch the triangle and mark the right angle.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_pyt_1_b">The hypotenuse lies opposite the right angle. Add the squares of the two legs.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_pyt_1_c">The sum of the squares is %1$s. The root is still missing.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_pyt_1_d">A leg is wanted: rearrange the theorem for it and subtract the square of the known leg.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_pyt_1_e">After subtracting, %1$s remains. The root is still missing.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_str_1_a">Draw both rays and the parallels; note which segment belongs to which.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_str_1_b">On both rays the segments are in the same ratio.</string>
    <string name="h_ge_str_1_c">The scale factor between the two parallels is %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_kla_1_a">The bracket as a whole is multiplied by %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_kla_1_b">First divide by the factor in front of the bracket; the bracket stays together.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_lgs_1_a">Two equations, two unknowns: one of them has to go first.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_lgs_1_b">Bring both equations to the same number of y and subtract them.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_lgs_1_c">After subtracting you get %1$sx = %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_lin_1_a">What is still in the way of the x?</string>
    <string name="h_gl_lin_1_b">First move the number to the other side, then divide by the factor.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_qua_1_a">Look for two numbers whose sum is the value in front of x and whose product is the last value.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_qua_1_b">The two numbers add up to %1$s and multiply to %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_qua_1_c">One of the two solutions is %1$s. The other one is wanted.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_ter_1_a">Substitute the number for x and write the term out again.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_ter_1_b">Multiplication before addition: multiply first, then add or subtract.</string>
    <string name="h_gl_zwischenschritt">This is what the equation looks like after the first step: %1$s</string>
    <string name="h_gr_add_1_a">Take the second addend in two portions.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_add_1_b">First %1$s on top, then the remaining %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_div_1_a">Dividing means multiplying backwards.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_div_1_b">Look for the number %2$s in the times table of %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_div_1_c">%1$s · %2$s = %3$s, that is still too small.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_mul_1_a">Use a row you know well as a stepping stone.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_mul_1_b">Compute %1$s · %2$s and add %1$s once more.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_mul_1_c">%1$s · %2$s = %3$s. One more %1$s is missing.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_mul_2_a">Split the larger number into tens and ones.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_mul_2_b">Compute %1$s · %2$s and %3$s · %2$s and add both.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_sub_1_a">Take the subtrahend in two portions.</string>
    <string name="h_gr_sub_1_b">First take away %1$s, then the remaining %2$s.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_add_1_a">Think of the number line.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_add_1_b">Plus means to the right, minus means to the left.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_add_1_c">From %1$s it is %2$s steps.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_mul_1_a">First compute without the signs.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_mul_1_b">Count the minus signs: an even number gives plus, an odd number minus.</string>
    <string name="h_nz_mul_1_c">The absolute value is %1$s, only the sign is missing.</string>
    <string name="h_po_rat_1_a">A fraction in the exponent is a root and a power in one.</string>
    <string name="h_po_rat_1_b">The denominator %1$s is the root, the numerator %2$s is the power.</string>
    <string name="h_po_rat_1_c">The root gives %1$s. Now comes the power.</string>
    <string name="h_po_wur_1_a">A square root asks: which number times itself gives this?</string>
    <string name="h_po_wur_1_b">Go through the square numbers until you find the right one.</string>
    <string name="h_po_wur_1_c">Take a closer look at %1$s.</string>
    <string name="h_pz_sat_1_a">First form the fraction of the share.</string>
    <string name="h_pz_sat_1_b">Percentage value divided by base value, then times 100.</string>
